Extension API: where the colony screen puts its buildings ("CBLD").

STATUS: NOT APPLIED — written by work order 180 (B3), parked for Data
(doc/briefs/180-parked-for-data.md, item 1.2).

PROOF (work order 180, 27 September 2026): this file is one step of a
series of six (open fixes 35-40, applied in that order on top of
orionlayer-local 9ab84230 — each appends its block after the previous
one). In a scratch clone of the engine, never on orionlayer-local:
`patch -p1 --dry-run` then `patch -p1` applied it cleanly on the steps
before it, and the patched src/ext/ext_api.cpp compiled with the engine's
own flags from out/build/Linux/linux-debug/build.ninja (the ORION2RE_EXT
and debug defines, every include directory, the cmake_pch.hxx include,
-fsyntax-only) — with a control: the same file with `_colony_satellites` misspelt `_colony_satelites` was
refused. A full build of the whole series ran in scratch, only to record
the wire format for OrionLayer's fixtures.

To apply, from ~/orion2re on the orionlayer-local branch, after ext_colony_screen_colony.patch (35):

    patch -p1 < ~/orionlayerv3/doc/ext_colony_building_placement.patch

then rebuild. It comes back off with `patch -R -p1` and a rebuild.

WHAT IT IS FOR

    COLONY::Make_Bldg_Array_For_Colony_ (colony_main.cpp:536-631) places
    the colony's buildings on a 6x6 grid (COLONY::_colony_bldgs) and its
    satellites in ten slots (_colony_satellites) with game_random seeded
    by the colony index and further Random_ draws; Draw_Colony_Bldgs_
    and Draw_Colony_Satellites_ draw from those arrays, and the roads
    follow them. WHICH buildings a colony has is on the wire
    (s_colony.buildings); WHERE they stand is not. A client copy would
    have to transcribe the RNG exactly — decision 25's "reconstruct
    first" meets a random number generator.

WIRE, after COLS, only while screen 1 is up:

    "CBLD"  36 x int16 building id, row by row (_colony_bldgs[r]
            .buildings[c]), then 10 x int16 _colony_satellites[i]

WHAT IT COSTS US WITHOUT IT: HD lists the colony's buildings and
satellites (a DEVIATION: a list, not the scene) and draws no roads and no
placement (HD STATE).

diff --git a/src/ext/ext_api.cpp b/src/ext/ext_api.cpp
index 868af44c..b057ad33 100644
--- a/src/ext/ext_api.cpp
+++ b/src/ext/ext_api.cpp
@@ -468,6 +468,31 @@ static void SerializeState(std::vector<uint8_t>& buf,
         Write8(buf, COLONY::_field_mode);
         Write8(buf, BUILD_QUEUE::autobuild_settings.enabled);
     }
+
+    // 7. Where the colony screen puts its buildings: "CBLD", written ONLY
+    //    while SCREEN_COLONY is up, and LAST. OrionLayer, open fix 36.
+    //
+    //    Make_Bldg_Array_For_Colony_ (colony_main.cpp:536-631) places the
+    //    colony's buildings on a 6x6 grid and its satellites in ten slots
+    //    with game_random seeded by the colony index plus Random_ draws;
+    //    Draw_Colony_Bldgs_ and Draw_Colony_Satellites_ draw from those two
+    //    arrays. A client would have to transcribe the RNG exactly, so the
+    //    arrays are sent as they are: 36 building ids, row by row, then the
+    //    ten satellite ids. Valid while COLS's handle agrees with its pair.
+    if (current_screen == SCREEN_COLONY) {
+        buf.push_back((uint8_t)'C');
+        buf.push_back((uint8_t)'B');
+        buf.push_back((uint8_t)'L');
+        buf.push_back((uint8_t)'D');
+        for (int r = 0; r < 6; r++) {
+            for (int c = 0; c < 6; c++) {
+                Write16(buf, (int16_t)COLONY::_colony_bldgs[r].buildings[c]);
+            }
+        }
+        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
+            Write16(buf, COLONY::_colony_satellites[i]);
+        }
+    }
 }
 
